Part I — Origins and historical development
3. How did the ten Mahāvidyās emerge?
The Mahāvidyās are best studied as a historically developing group of Goddess traditions, rather than as a list whose entire history can be traced to one original text or event.

Their individual members have different histories. Kālī, Tārā, Tripurasundarī, and other Goddesses developed in distinct religious and regional settings before and during the consolidation of the tenfold grouping.

Modern scholarship treats the Mahāvidyās as a synthesis of several strands of religious history, including Kālī worship, Śrīvidyā, Yoginī traditions, Śaiva and Śākta Tantra, and other currents of Indian religious culture. 
