6. Kālī — कालī







sonaom.medium.com


Identity and theology
Kālī is frequently placed first in contemporary lists of the ten Mahāvidyās. Her name is connected with kāla, meaning time, and she is widely associated with mortality, transformation, fierce compassion, and the dissolution of manifested forms.

In Kālī-centered Śākta theology, she may be understood as a supreme manifestation of the Goddess. In some nondual traditions, she is associated with the power through which consciousness manifests and withdraws the universe.

These interpretations differ between texts and lineages. Kālī is not merely a personification of destruction: her fierce imagery can coexist with maternal devotion, protection, and the promise of liberation.
Iconography
Common representations include:
- A dark or black complexion.
- Disheveled hair and a fierce expression.
- A garland of severed heads or skulls.
- A girdle of severed arms in some forms.
- Weapons, including a sword or cleaver, and a severed head in certain iconographic traditions.
- A posture standing upon Śiva in widely recognized depictions.
Not every image contains all these features. Their meanings depend on the particular form of Kālī and the source used to interpret it.
Mythology and symbolic interpretation
Kālī appears in different mythological settings, including accounts of conflict with demonic forces and stories in which her destructive activity becomes overwhelming. Some devotional narratives describe Śiva intervening to halt her movement.

The relationship between Kālī and Śiva has also been interpreted philosophically through the relationship between consciousness and its dynamic power. Such interpretations should be distinguished from the narrative meanings found in individual myths.
