9. Bhuvaneśvarī — भुवनेश्वरी







tantrasadhana.app


Identity and theology
Bhuvaneśvarī's name is commonly understood as “Queen” or “Sovereign of the Worlds.” She represents the Goddess in relation to the cosmos and the domain in which existence unfolds.

Her theological significance is often associated with the vastness of manifestation. The worlds are not merely objects over which she rules; they can also be understood as expressions of her power.
Iconography
Common depictions show Bhuvaneśvarī as a beautiful, regal Goddess with a radiant complexion, elaborate ornaments, and multiple arms. In familiar forms, she may hold a noose and goad while making gestures of reassurance and blessing.

Specific attributes vary between iconographic traditions.
Philosophical themes
Bhuvaneśvarī's name invites a connection between divine sovereignty and the space or expanse of the universe. This can be used as an interpretive framework for understanding manifestation as an expression of Śakti.

It is important, however, to distinguish a symbolic interpretation of her name from a technical metaphysical doctrine attributed to a particular text.
Relationship to the other Mahāvidyās
Bhuvaneśvarī contributes a cosmic and sovereign dimension to the tenfold group. Where Kālī is often associated with time and dissolution, Bhuvaneśvarī is frequently interpreted through the expansive domain in which manifested existence appears.

This is a comparative framework, not an absolute division between two separate cosmic functions.
