16. The ten Goddesses as a unified theological system
One of the most important questions is whether the Mahāvidyās are ten independent Goddesses or ten manifestations of a single supreme Goddess.

Many Śākta accounts emphasize their unity. In these traditions, the Goddesses are distinct in appearance, mythology, and ritual identity, yet understood as expressions of the same supreme power.

However, this unity is not explained identically everywhere. Some accounts place Kālī at the center; others give prominence to Tripurasundarī or a broader conception of the supreme Goddess. The theological framework depends on the text and tradition. 
