17. The fierce and auspicious forms
A popular way of introducing the Mahāvidyās is to distinguish between fierce forms and forms associated with beauty, prosperity, or benevolence.

This can be useful as an initial guide to iconography, but it is not a universally standardized division.

For example:
- Kālī may be fierce in iconography while also being worshipped as a protective and loving mother.
- Tripurasundarī may appear beautiful and auspicious while belonging to a demanding Tantric tradition.
- Mātaṅgī may have elegant imagery while engaging with socially unconventional themes.
- Kamalā may embody prosperity without being reducible to material wealth alone.
The group becomes more meaningful when these apparent contrasts are studied together. It brings together aspects of existence that conventional religious aesthetics might otherwise separate. 
